WEBVTT

00:01.001 --> 00:04.962
<i>♪ It seems today that all you see ♪</i>

00:04.963 --> 00:08.174
<i>♪ Is violence in movies and sex on TV ♪</i>

00:08.175 --> 00:11.511
<i>♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪</i>

00:11.512 --> 00:14.472
<i>♪ On which we used to rely? ♪</i>

00:14.473 --> 00:18.017
<i>♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪</i>

00:18.018 --> 00:21.604
<i>♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪</i>

00:21.605 --> 00:23.189
<i>♪ All the things that make us ♪</i>

00:23.190 --> 00:24.690
<i>♪ Laugh and cry ♪</i>

00:24.691 --> 00:30.155
<i>♪ He's... a... Fam... ily... Guy! ♪</i>

00:35.077 --> 00:37.745
<i>We now return to LEGO Spotlight.</i>

00:42.459 --> 00:45.151
I'm not sure we should be
letting Stewie watch this.

00:45.152 --> 00:46.546
It's LEGO. It's fine.

00:46.547 --> 00:49.090
Yeah, you're right.

00:49.091 --> 00:50.424
Well, that's my train.

00:50.425 --> 00:53.386
Time to disappear
from this life completely.

00:53.387 --> 00:56.222
No, no, Peter. It's just Chris
practicing his new recorder.

00:56.223 --> 00:58.769
What? I said no hobbies.

00:59.226 --> 01:01.727
Brian, that LEGO movie
kind of scared me.

01:01.728 --> 01:04.480
Ah, don't worry about it,
Stewie. It's all make-believe.

01:04.481 --> 01:07.401
Ah! LEGO! LEGO!

01:10.821 --> 01:13.089
Chris, stop! That sounds terrible.

01:13.090 --> 01:15.074
Yeah, you can't just play
one note over and over.

01:15.075 --> 01:16.742
You're not Danny McBride.

01:16.743 --> 01:18.411
Who taught you to play like that?

01:18.412 --> 01:20.079
Well, our music teacher quit

01:20.080 --> 01:22.540
and was replaced by a back-East bird.

01:29.548 --> 01:31.090
See you tomorrow, Mr. Mason.

01:32.968 --> 01:35.511
Well, I'm gonna have to talk to
Principal Shepherd about this.

01:35.512 --> 01:38.014
Great. Every kid loves seeing their mom

01:38.015 --> 01:39.348
inside their high school.

01:39.349 --> 01:41.434
I'm sorry, but music
is an important part

01:41.435 --> 01:42.893
of your education.

01:42.894 --> 01:44.061
Your mother's right, Chris.

01:44.062 --> 01:45.187
You don't want to mess this up,

01:45.188 --> 01:47.231
like Skee-Io messed up his wishes.

01:47.232 --> 01:50.109
Uh, I wish I was a little bit
taller, I wish I was a baller,

01:50.110 --> 01:52.278
I wish I had a rabbit
in a hat with a bat.

01:54.740 --> 01:57.116
- Skee-Io?!
- What?

01:57.117 --> 01:59.536
You need money!

02:03.790 --> 02:06.917
You wanted to see me, Mrs. Griffin?

02:06.918 --> 02:09.920
Um, yes, to discuss the music program.

02:09.921 --> 02:11.922
Right! Right, of course.

02:11.923 --> 02:14.634
Sorry. I was just testing
these... candles

02:14.635 --> 02:17.720
for a... lie I can't...
think of right now.

02:17.721 --> 02:21.641
Now, what's this forgettable
misunderstanding all about?

02:21.642 --> 02:24.632
Uh, my son is being taught by a bird.

02:24.633 --> 02:26.979
- Ah. Ms. Wilkins.
- No, Mr. Mason.

02:26.980 --> 02:29.565
Right. Lot of open windows
in this school.

02:29.566 --> 02:31.817
You need a real music teacher.

02:31.818 --> 02:35.321
I'm a trained piano player, and
I've taught lessons for years.

02:35.322 --> 02:37.070
I'd love to come work for you.

02:37.071 --> 02:39.951
You would?
Well, that changes everything.

02:42.287 --> 02:44.330
I'm not gonna have sex with you.

02:44.331 --> 02:47.209
I know. I just farted.

02:50.671 --> 02:54.632
So, everyone,
I have an announcement to make.

02:54.633 --> 02:57.843
I'm gonna be the new
music teacher at Adam West High.

02:57.844 --> 02:59.595
- What?
- Please say you're joking.

02:59.596 --> 03:01.806
I'm not joking. I start Monday.

03:01.807 --> 03:04.100
My wife, a music teacher?

03:04.101 --> 03:06.687
Hold on. That's a marriage time-out.

03:12.693 --> 03:14.652
I... I think Dad did this wrong.

03:14.653 --> 03:17.029
<i>Yeah, aren't-aren't we supposed
to be the frozen ones?</i>

03:17.030 --> 03:19.365
- Dad's a loser.
- Yeah, he sucks.

03:19.366 --> 03:21.784
- Stop it.
- Why? He can't hear us.

03:21.785 --> 03:23.160
<i>But I could hear them.</i>

03:23.161 --> 03:24.662
<i>And it hurt.</i>

03:24.663 --> 03:25.871
<i>Turns out, if you're gonna learn</i>

03:25.872 --> 03:28.040
<i>how to stop time in wizard school,</i>

03:28.041 --> 03:30.960
<i>don't leave class early to be
the first in line for lunch.</i>

03:30.961 --> 03:32.712
<i>It was pizza day.</i>

03:32.713 --> 03:34.714
<i>And you'd think it's got
to be pretty good pizza,</i>

03:34.715 --> 03:37.258
<i>being made by a magic wand and whatnot.</i>

03:37.259 --> 03:39.051
<i>But it was just okay.</i>

03:39.052 --> 03:41.679
<i>I don't think
it was the usual guy making it.</i>

03:41.680 --> 03:45.475
<i>This has been a Peter Griffin
Marriage Time-Out.</i>

03:53.694 --> 03:55.736
Good morning, class.

03:57.070 --> 04:00.282
So... you know.

04:01.825 --> 04:04.702
I'm your new music teacher,
Mrs. Griffin,

04:04.703 --> 04:07.913
and I started from the bottom.
Now I'm here.

04:07.914 --> 04:10.082
So... rap.

04:11.585 --> 04:13.461
Oh, my God, you're so lame.

04:13.462 --> 04:15.212
I know you are, but what am I?

04:15.213 --> 04:16.547
Lame.

04:16.548 --> 04:18.674
Okay, we'll call that one a tie.

04:18.675 --> 04:21.510
Now, class, what is music to you?

04:21.511 --> 04:22.762
Nothing.

04:22.763 --> 04:24.263
Okay, Deaf Larry, we know

04:24.264 --> 04:26.474
music is nothing to you
and never will be.

04:26.475 --> 04:28.184
Who else? What is music to you?

04:28.185 --> 04:29.351
Stupid.

04:29.352 --> 04:31.562
Connie, you're just afraid,
so you lash out.

04:31.563 --> 04:34.106
<i>I get you, because I was you.</i>

04:34.107 --> 04:36.734
Headstrong, full of anger,
and a body that looked

04:36.735 --> 04:38.944
<i>like it stepped
right out of Redbook magazine.</i>

04:38.945 --> 04:40.780
- Is that good?
- Yes, that's good.

04:40.781 --> 04:42.156
You don't know. Shut up.

04:42.157 --> 04:43.868
<i>You were never me.</i>

04:43.869 --> 04:46.495
Oh, no? Look down at your desk.

04:48.330 --> 04:49.622
"L.G."?

04:49.623 --> 04:51.373
Lois... Griffin.

04:51.374 --> 04:54.126
But-but weren't you
Lois Pewterschmidt back then?

04:54.127 --> 04:55.711
Meg, go to the principal's office.

04:55.712 --> 04:57.379
My desk also has "L.G." on it.

04:57.380 --> 04:58.879
- Mine, too.
- They all do!

04:58.880 --> 05:02.301
Okay, okay, I didn't know where
the troublemaker was gonna sit.

05:02.302 --> 05:05.971
Mr. Mason may have been a bird,
but at least he wasn't a liar.

05:05.972 --> 05:09.518
<i>Well, you better get used to me,
'cause Mr. Mason's gone.</i>

05:11.311 --> 05:13.771
Sweetie, it's the middle
of the day. What happened?

05:13.772 --> 05:16.148
Oh, no.

05:16.149 --> 05:19.777
Well, I hope this doesn't affect
our plan to have eggs.

05:21.154 --> 05:23.907
<i>You wanted this to happen!</i>

05:28.703 --> 05:31.121
So, Lois, how was
your first big day teaching?

05:31.122 --> 05:32.581
You pull a Mary Kay Letourneau?

05:34.000 --> 05:35.334
No, Peter.

05:35.335 --> 05:36.794
I didn't leave my family

05:36.795 --> 05:40.673
to have sex with a 12-year-old
boy named Vili Fualaau,

05:40.674 --> 05:44.343
get arrested for rape, give
birth to his child in prison,

05:44.344 --> 05:46.846
get out, get arrested again
for humping him in a car

05:46.847 --> 05:49.682
when he was 13,
have his second kid in jail,

05:49.683 --> 05:51.308
get out, get married to him,

05:51.309 --> 05:54.520
change my name
to Mary Kay Letourneau-Fualaau,

05:54.521 --> 05:56.856
live my life
as a disgraced sex offender,

05:56.857 --> 05:58.691
and then get dumped by him.

05:58.692 --> 06:01.443
No, I didn't do that.

06:01.444 --> 06:04.029
But I did have a little trouble
in Meg's class

06:04.030 --> 06:06.240
because of that brat Connie D'Amico.

06:06.241 --> 06:09.076
She's a classic,
entitled, mean-girl slut.

06:09.077 --> 06:11.662
Connie D'Amico? She stopped by
to see you yesterday.

06:11.663 --> 06:13.372
She did?! What'd she say?

06:13.373 --> 06:16.208
- She said you're gullible.
- And?

06:16.209 --> 06:18.417
Hmm, Connie D'Amico.

06:18.418 --> 06:19.837
The name sounds familiar.

06:19.838 --> 06:21.547
I better go check Meg's yearbook...

06:21.548 --> 06:25.384
Pages four, six, 11,
27, 41, 42 and 60...

06:25.385 --> 06:27.887
To see if it's the babe I'm thinking of.

06:27.888 --> 06:29.388
Lois, I'll take my dessert upstairs

06:29.389 --> 06:31.849
in the masturbation bathroom.

06:31.850 --> 06:33.559
Maybe I'm not cut out

06:33.560 --> 06:35.227
to teach high school kids.

06:35.228 --> 06:37.354
I thought I could handle their nonsense,

06:37.355 --> 06:39.523
but I just kind of froze today
with Connie.

06:39.524 --> 06:41.942
- Yeah, hang it up.
- You gave it a day.

06:41.943 --> 06:43.110
That's nonsense, Lois.

06:43.111 --> 06:45.195
You just have to be more
of an authority figure.

06:45.196 --> 06:46.572
If Connie gets out of line again,

06:46.573 --> 06:48.407
don't be afraid to show her who's boss.

06:48.408 --> 06:50.326
Like I did with those squirrels.

06:50.327 --> 06:52.328
Hey, neighborinos.

06:52.329 --> 06:54.079
Um, so, not a huge deal,

06:54.080 --> 06:56.248
but if you guys could keep
your chittering down,

06:56.249 --> 06:58.584
I'm trying to get
some writing done in the house.

06:58.585 --> 07:00.446
Oh, yeah, yeah, no-no problem, bro.

07:00.447 --> 07:02.546
Also, maybe you could go (BLEEP)
your own face, huh?

07:02.547 --> 07:03.756
Could that be good?

07:03.757 --> 07:04.798
Yeah!

07:06.259 --> 07:08.093
Oh. Okay.

07:08.094 --> 07:10.429
All right, thanks, guys.

07:10.430 --> 07:12.181
Stewie? What are you doing here?

07:12.182 --> 07:13.891
I followed you into your cutaway

07:13.892 --> 07:16.268
because I didn't believe
you showed anyone who's boss.

07:16.269 --> 07:17.770
And I was right.

07:17.771 --> 07:20.460
Did you get a sleeve tattoo
of Freddie Mercury?

07:20.461 --> 07:22.192
Yeah, it's a cutaway.

07:26.738 --> 07:29.782
I know some of you kids think
this class has nothing to do

07:29.783 --> 07:32.868
with your day-to-day lives,
but you couldn't be more wrong.

07:32.869 --> 07:35.788
Music tells the story of our lives.

07:35.789 --> 07:37.790
For example, this morning,

07:37.791 --> 07:40.709
I woke up and saw it was a sunny day.

07:45.632 --> 07:47.716
Then I had some tea.

07:49.803 --> 07:52.429
Which was from China.

07:52.430 --> 07:56.308
On my way out the door, the
kitty cat ran across my piano.

07:58.269 --> 07:59.728
Get off of there, kitty cat!

08:01.314 --> 08:03.148
Then I drove to work.

08:05.986 --> 08:08.320
But then, I hit some traffic...

08:10.991 --> 08:13.409
that was caused by a parade.

08:16.079 --> 08:17.663
For gay rights.

08:20.291 --> 08:22.835
And finally, I got to school early

08:22.836 --> 08:25.838
and diarrhea'd
in the girls' locker room.

08:28.633 --> 08:30.050
The end.

08:30.051 --> 08:31.135
You suck!

08:31.136 --> 08:32.302
Aah!

08:35.640 --> 08:37.516
That's it, Connie!

08:37.517 --> 08:39.310
To the principal's office. Now!

08:43.565 --> 08:47.358
Okay. For the rest of the class,
I will wait for the bell.

08:48.653 --> 08:50.904
And then I will suddenly talk
much louder and faster

08:50.905 --> 08:52.489
as you're heading out the door.

08:52.490 --> 08:55.034
Okay, and remember, there's
a quiz a week from Wednesday!

08:55.035 --> 08:56.827
Oh, no.

08:56.828 --> 08:58.704
Good. That means you heard me.

08:58.705 --> 09:02.000
This is how Hollywood thinks
school teachers talk.

09:05.670 --> 09:07.671
Connie, I've asked Mrs. Griffin
to be here

09:07.672 --> 09:09.840
<i>since it was her class you disrupted.</i>

09:09.841 --> 09:13.218
And I'm sorry to say, this is
your third strike this year.

09:13.219 --> 09:15.012
According to school policy,

09:15.013 --> 09:17.264
three strikes means you're expelled.

09:17.265 --> 09:19.265
- What?!
- Principal Shepherd,

09:19.266 --> 09:21.060
I'm not sure that's necessary.

09:21.061 --> 09:23.145
I'm sorry. I've made my decision.

09:23.146 --> 09:25.397
Now, if you ladies don't mind
stepping out,

09:25.398 --> 09:27.441
I have to reprimand Danny McBride next.

09:27.442 --> 09:29.735
Mr. McBride,
do you know why you're here?

09:29.736 --> 09:33.697
Yeah, maybe because I'm awesome,
and you're a gay homosexual,

09:33.698 --> 09:35.407
and also the word "Jet Ski" a lot?

09:36.910 --> 09:39.537
Yeah, you know why you're here.

09:45.293 --> 09:48.212
Ugh. I feel kind of bad
about getting Connie expelled.

09:48.213 --> 09:49.922
But maybe this'll make her realize

09:49.923 --> 09:52.007
she needs to be
more respectful to others.

09:52.008 --> 09:54.009
Eh, probably not. She's really hot.

09:55.261 --> 09:56.929
Hey, Lois, I just got an invite

09:56.930 --> 09:58.680
for a Facebook group you started called

09:58.681 --> 10:01.350
"Keep Harriet Tubman
Off the 20-Dollar Bill."

10:01.351 --> 10:03.811
What? I haven't even been
on Facebook today.

10:03.812 --> 10:06.522
Wow. Your profile pic is now
just the word "veterans"

10:06.523 --> 10:08.899
with a circle and a line through it.

10:08.900 --> 10:11.485
I mean, that's the way I feel
about ghosts, but veterans?

10:11.486 --> 10:13.612
Mom, this is Connie getting back at you.

10:13.613 --> 10:15.280
It's called "cyberbullying."

10:15.281 --> 10:18.117
Cyberbullying? Me?

10:18.118 --> 10:21.995
By the way, Vili Fualaau
is now a DJ in Seattle.

10:21.996 --> 10:24.081
Boy, Connie must really mean business.

10:24.082 --> 10:26.750
Like an Armenian kid in a candy store.

10:26.751 --> 10:29.086
Hey, little boy, can I help you?

10:29.087 --> 10:30.712
Yes, I wish to buy business

10:30.713 --> 10:33.340
and turn into car wash.

10:38.972 --> 10:42.225
We had no idea
you'd switched teams, Lois.

10:46.354 --> 10:48.439
Ugh. Damn it, Connie.

10:48.440 --> 10:50.482
No, it's just a nasty prank.

10:50.483 --> 10:52.234
What's so nasty about it?

10:52.235 --> 10:53.986
I mean, nothing.

10:53.987 --> 10:55.571
I-I'm fine with it.

10:55.572 --> 10:57.614
Okay, well, if you're so fine with it,

10:57.615 --> 10:59.116
what are you doing Friday night?

10:59.117 --> 11:01.451
Oh, um...

11:01.452 --> 11:04.663
Mmm. So, I got
my blacksmith certification.

11:04.664 --> 11:07.416
- I can make axes now.
- Oh.

11:07.417 --> 11:09.376
You in the market for an ax?

11:09.377 --> 11:11.503
I'll keep this on the D.I. Hughley,

11:11.504 --> 11:14.883
but your next date should be
with Christ.

11:17.886 --> 11:20.929
Lois, are you using the Elmo toothbrush,

11:20.930 --> 11:23.432
- which is mine?
- Oh, sorry, Peter.

11:23.433 --> 11:26.185
I've just been so distracted
with this whole Connie thing.

11:26.186 --> 11:27.944
Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm.

11:27.945 --> 11:30.355
So, Connie made you mistake
a monster's face

11:30.356 --> 11:31.732
for the words "Oral-B."

11:31.733 --> 11:33.192
Come on, Peter, I'm serious.

11:33.193 --> 11:34.693
This is a real problem.

11:34.694 --> 11:36.111
Well, if she's really ruining your life,

11:36.112 --> 11:37.696
go down to the station and talk to Joe.

11:37.697 --> 11:39.781
Yeah, I thought of that,
but I hate competing

11:39.782 --> 11:43.076
with the sound of ringing phones
and typewriter keys.

11:43.077 --> 11:44.203
And she hacked

11:44.204 --> 11:46.371
- all my Internet accounts.
- What?

11:46.372 --> 11:49.333
Connie! I think she hacked my Twitter!

11:49.334 --> 11:51.335
Well, without any hard evidence, Lois,

11:51.336 --> 11:53.545
I'm afraid there's nothing we can do!

11:53.546 --> 11:56.798
Fine! I knew this would be
a waste of time!

11:56.799 --> 11:59.551
Okay, fellas, she's gone!

12:01.095 --> 12:03.555
I'd like to report somebody
using the wrong toothbrush.

12:03.556 --> 12:05.224
Have a seat, sir. Tell me everything.

12:05.225 --> 12:07.393
Okay. You know Elmo, right?

12:11.064 --> 12:14.316
Hmm, I'm not seeing
any Viva brand paper towels.

12:14.317 --> 12:16.902
- That's the only brand I use.
- Uh, sounds good.

12:16.903 --> 12:19.780
I'm gonna go see what new stuff
they're turning into milk.

12:19.781 --> 12:22.074
Oh, wood milk!

12:22.075 --> 12:25.619
Hey, that woman has
all the Viva brand paper towels.

12:25.620 --> 12:27.829
E-Excuse me, miss,

12:27.830 --> 12:31.125
could I have one of those
Viva brand paper towel rolls?

12:32.835 --> 12:35.712
Oh, so sorry.

12:35.713 --> 12:38.758
I need them all
for the big mess you've made.

12:38.759 --> 12:40.842
Hey, Lois, do we need
any jeans milk at home?

12:40.843 --> 12:43.345
Oh, hey, Connie. Nice shopping outfit.

12:43.346 --> 12:44.596
Come on, Connie.

12:44.597 --> 12:46.223
It's the only brand I use.

12:46.224 --> 12:49.518
I need it to pat down bacon
and let Meg suck the wad.

12:49.519 --> 12:50.936
Too bad, bitch.

13:03.449 --> 13:05.492
There we go.

13:05.493 --> 13:07.786
I know it's been you cyberbullying me.

13:07.787 --> 13:09.288
I didn't mean to get you expelled,

13:09.289 --> 13:10.956
but now I'm glad I did.

13:10.957 --> 13:13.417
You're a very disrespectful young lady.

13:13.418 --> 13:15.085
<i>At least I'll have a paper towel</i>

13:15.086 --> 13:17.337
with the power to take on any spill.

13:17.338 --> 13:18.588
Okay, that's it.

13:18.589 --> 13:20.215
You'll pay for this, Connie!

13:20.216 --> 13:23.635
You better watch your step.

13:29.600 --> 13:31.768
Ah, darn it. I couldn't open
my camera in time.

13:31.769 --> 13:33.979
Oh, you can just text me yours.

13:33.980 --> 13:36.774
I'm not texting you. I don't know you.

13:39.777 --> 13:41.945
Peter, I'm exhausted.

13:41.946 --> 13:43.739
I can't even think of making dinner.

13:43.740 --> 13:45.324
Well, I didn't hear what you just said,

13:45.325 --> 13:46.992
but what's the dinner situation?

13:48.911 --> 13:51.747
<i>Good evening, I'm Tom Tucker,
transitioning into my sad voice</i>

13:51.748 --> 13:53.415
<i>for the death of a white girl.</i>

13:53.416 --> 13:56.168
<i>Local white girl Connie D'Amico
died at the scene</i>

13:56.169 --> 13:58.337
<i>of an automobile accident
earlier tonight,</i>

13:58.338 --> 14:01.465
<i>the brake lines of Connie's
Celica apparently cut.</i>

14:01.466 --> 14:03.800
Oh, my God. How horrible!

14:03.801 --> 14:05.802
Can you imagine being the shovel

14:05.803 --> 14:08.972
that gets to scrape that ass
off the road?

14:08.973 --> 14:11.433
Eh, maybe in my next life.

14:13.478 --> 14:16.646
Lois Griffin, I'm sorry,
but you're under arrest.

14:16.647 --> 14:19.316
I'm gonna have to take you down
to the station.

14:19.317 --> 14:20.984
On what charge?

14:20.985 --> 14:22.778
We've got you on grocery store video

14:22.779 --> 14:24.321
threatening Connie D'Amico.

14:24.322 --> 14:26.198
You're under arrest for her murder.

14:26.199 --> 14:27.574
What?!

14:27.575 --> 14:31.119
Guys, my mom was arrested
for Connie's murder.

14:31.120 --> 14:33.038
No quiz!

14:44.550 --> 14:45.884
Joe, this is ridiculous.

14:45.885 --> 14:47.677
There's no way Lois killed Connie.

14:47.678 --> 14:49.346
There's just too much evidence
against her.

14:49.347 --> 14:50.931
I got to take her in.

14:50.932 --> 14:53.364
So, what, I'm supposed to be
a guy without a wife now?

14:53.365 --> 14:56.211
What am I supposed to do,
whatever I want whenever I want?

14:56.212 --> 14:58.230
I'm gonna get crow's-feet
from smiling too much.

14:58.231 --> 15:00.565
Sorry, Peter. It's gonna be
quite an adjustment.

15:00.566 --> 15:03.026
Seriously, who's gonna greet me
in a shapeless sweatshirt

15:03.027 --> 15:05.028
- when I come home?
- Meg, maybe.

15:05.029 --> 15:07.572
Who's gonna slap a tarantula
of shampoo hair

15:07.573 --> 15:08.907
on the wall of the shower?

15:08.908 --> 15:10.367
It's the little things you miss.

15:10.368 --> 15:12.661
Who's gonna go, "Ooh! Ooh!
Careful!" while I'm parking?

15:12.662 --> 15:15.163
Sometimes this is
the hardest part of my job.

15:15.164 --> 15:18.041
Who's gonna fill up my DVR
with lady judge programs?

15:18.042 --> 15:19.501
I-I don't make the rules.

15:19.502 --> 15:22.003
Oh, and I suppose now I'll have
to start every road trip

15:22.004 --> 15:24.506
- with a full tank of gas?
- I took an oath, Peter.

15:24.507 --> 15:26.842
What am I supposed to do?
Masturbate comfortably in bed?

15:26.843 --> 15:28.218
The law's the law.

15:28.219 --> 15:30.595
And I guess I'm gonna have to be
on time to every party

15:30.596 --> 15:33.014
- and stay real late?
- Yeah, why do they do that?

15:33.015 --> 15:35.225
<i>And so long, Handmaid's Tale, I guess.</i>

15:35.226 --> 15:37.018
Two or three more jokes, Peter.

15:37.019 --> 15:38.353
<i>So long, This Is Us.</i>

15:38.354 --> 15:39.521
Last one, maybe.

15:39.522 --> 15:41.398
So long, seeing my dining room.

15:41.399 --> 15:42.774
You got a stronger one to end on?

15:42.775 --> 15:45.235
So long, bitchy 2:00 a.m. blanket pulls.

15:45.236 --> 15:46.611
Ah, there we go.

15:46.612 --> 15:49.240
(BLEEP), Peter.

15:50.950 --> 15:52.409
What am I supposed to do now, Brian?

15:52.410 --> 15:54.369
Whatever I want whenever I want?

15:54.370 --> 15:56.704
Yeah, I was right here
when you were doing that.

15:56.705 --> 15:58.623
Oh. I-I didn't hear your laugh.

15:58.624 --> 16:00.710
I'm more of a smile guy.

16:04.297 --> 16:06.631
Peter, we have got to prove
Lois is innocent.

16:06.632 --> 16:08.550
That's right. And the best way to crack

16:08.551 --> 16:10.469
any high-profile crime case

16:10.470 --> 16:13.555
is to do a podcast
before you have all the facts.

16:13.556 --> 16:15.432
- Okay.
- And the key to any podcast

16:15.433 --> 16:18.268
is poor sound quality
and tons of commercials.

16:18.269 --> 16:19.895
All right, we've got Lois
calling in from prison.

16:19.896 --> 16:21.646
She's only got three minutes.

16:21.647 --> 16:23.648
<i>Thanks for looking at my case, guys.</i>

16:23.649 --> 16:25.567
You bet, Lois. My first question is...

16:25.568 --> 16:27.319
do you get a good night's sleep?

16:27.320 --> 16:30.405
<i>No, 'cause I'm in prison,
and my cellmate is bipolar.</i>

16:30.406 --> 16:32.491
Well, if you get a Casper mattress now,

16:32.492 --> 16:34.159
you have a hundred days to decide

16:34.160 --> 16:35.619
if it's the right mattress for you.

16:35.620 --> 16:37.329
<i>Peter, I don't have time for this.</i>

16:37.330 --> 16:39.331
I don't have time
for underwear that bunches up.

16:39.332 --> 16:42.000
That's why I wear MeUndies.
Perfect underwear

16:42.001 --> 16:43.752
for hanging yourself in your jail cell.

16:43.753 --> 16:46.505
<i>Can we talk about the case?
I have 30 seconds</i>

16:46.506 --> 16:49.007
<i>before they haul me off
to the freaking cafeteria.</i>

16:49.008 --> 16:50.926
Sounds like you could use Blue Apron.

16:50.927 --> 16:53.512
Have you ever wanted to receive
a vegetable in the mail?

16:53.513 --> 16:55.096
Then you need Blue Apron.

16:55.097 --> 16:56.973
I'm, like, obsessed with the stir-fry.

16:56.974 --> 16:59.142
Brian here likes the Moroccan beef.

16:59.143 --> 17:02.062
I-I have no opinion
on the Moroccan beef, Peter.

17:02.063 --> 17:04.147
Brian, this is not art.
This is commerce.

17:04.148 --> 17:06.695
<i>Peter, before I go, I need a new lawyer.</i>

17:06.696 --> 17:08.527
<i>This one made a sexual pass at me.</i>

17:08.528 --> 17:11.029
Oh, boy. Everyone knows
hiring is a nightmare.

17:11.030 --> 17:13.522
At least it was before ZipRecruiter.

17:13.523 --> 17:16.201
Ziprecruiter: We vastly overestimate

17:16.202 --> 17:19.705
how many podcast listeners are
in a position to hire someone.

17:23.084 --> 17:24.668
Okay, Brian,
let's go through Lois's drawers

17:24.669 --> 17:27.045
and look for clues.
If you find any, let me know.

17:27.046 --> 17:29.172
And if you find
the yo-yo she took from me,

17:29.173 --> 17:30.882
definitely let me know.

17:30.883 --> 17:32.050
Stupid Lois.

17:32.051 --> 17:33.986
I'm not gonna use it in the house.

17:33.987 --> 17:35.512
The Wendy's employee gave it to me

17:35.513 --> 17:37.097
if I promised to calm down.

17:37.098 --> 17:38.348
Peter, this is a dead end.

17:38.349 --> 17:40.600
All I see here is your Nerf gun
from last summer.

17:40.601 --> 17:42.978
Oh, no way. She said I lost that.

17:42.979 --> 17:45.272
She deserves to be in jail.

17:45.273 --> 17:46.356
Hey!

17:46.357 --> 17:48.024
Suck on that, Lois!

17:48.025 --> 17:50.151
That was very close to my eyes, Peter.

17:50.152 --> 17:51.820
It wasn't close to your eye.

17:51.821 --> 17:54.991
- That's why I took it.
- It was so far from your eye.

17:58.327 --> 17:59.494
What are we doing here?

17:59.495 --> 18:01.371
Connie never went to the Drunken Clam.

18:01.372 --> 18:03.873
Doesn't matter. Everybody knows
the way you solve a murder

18:03.874 --> 18:06.042
is to ask a barkeep
if he knows anything.

18:06.043 --> 18:07.836
When he says no, you turn around,

18:07.837 --> 18:10.589
and then he says "wait"
and gives you an important clue.

18:10.590 --> 18:13.425
Hey, Jerome, do you know
anything about Connie's death?

18:13.426 --> 18:16.011
No, man. I've just been working here.

18:16.012 --> 18:17.554
All right, Brian. Let's get out of here.

18:17.555 --> 18:19.598
- Peter, wait.
- Yes?

18:19.599 --> 18:22.726
One of the waitresses says
you put her tip down her pants.

18:22.727 --> 18:26.363
Huh. How long were you planning
to sit on that little nugget?

18:26.364 --> 18:27.564
You just did it.

18:27.565 --> 18:28.690
Okay, Jerome.

18:28.691 --> 18:31.219
<i>If you remember anything else,</i>

18:31.220 --> 18:32.656
here's my number.

18:33.446 --> 18:36.774
Oh, and here's a nickel
for the watered-down beer.

18:37.783 --> 18:39.827
Keep the change.

18:43.497 --> 18:45.332
How are we gonna solve this case, Peter?

18:45.333 --> 18:46.458
I've been thinking about that.

18:46.459 --> 18:49.093
You're a dog, and I'm a man. Right?

18:49.094 --> 18:50.612
<i>So we're basically Scooby-Doo.</i>

18:50.613 --> 18:52.464
- So, what are you saying?
- We go around town

18:52.465 --> 18:55.467
and rip off faces
until we find the real killer.

18:55.468 --> 18:57.052
Unless...

18:59.055 --> 19:00.847
Which can only mean...

19:02.350 --> 19:04.476
Twins? Guess again.

19:15.780 --> 19:18.866
The newspaper is embarrassing me.

19:23.781 --> 19:26.310
Well, kids, we tried.

19:26.311 --> 19:28.083
I'm afraid your mother's a murderer.

19:28.084 --> 19:30.168
No, Peter, I'm right here.

19:30.169 --> 19:32.215
Lois, you escaped!

19:32.216 --> 19:33.630
Careful, kids. She killed before.

19:33.631 --> 19:36.007
- She'll kill again.
- No, Peter, look wider.

19:36.008 --> 19:37.759
We're all here.

19:37.760 --> 19:39.344
Turns out Lois didn't kill anyone.

19:39.345 --> 19:41.805
Connie D'Amico faked her own death.

19:41.806 --> 19:43.306
Gasp!

19:43.307 --> 19:46.184
We found Connie an hour ago
at a motel outside of town.

19:46.185 --> 19:48.436
Turns out she put a pig in a blonde wig

19:48.437 --> 19:50.263
behind the wheel of her car.

19:50.264 --> 19:53.274
I'm sorry, our coroner
has been pretty sloppy lately.

19:53.275 --> 19:55.777
She's been dealing
with some substance abuse.

19:55.778 --> 19:58.029
"She"? Interesting.

19:58.030 --> 20:00.365
Anyway, Lois has been cleared
of all charges.

20:00.366 --> 20:01.866
And what of the pig?

20:01.867 --> 20:04.286
That was a very tough house call.

20:08.374 --> 20:10.792
- Yes?
- Excuse me, folks.

20:10.793 --> 20:12.883
Do you have a daughter named Peppa?

20:12.884 --> 20:14.045
Yes.

20:14.046 --> 20:16.507
You're gonna want to sit down.

20:23.347 --> 20:24.639
Well, Lois, I got to say,

20:24.640 --> 20:26.391
it's great to have you home from prison.

20:26.392 --> 20:27.851
Ah, it's great to be home.

20:27.852 --> 20:29.394
Hey, I-I got to ask,

20:29.395 --> 20:31.555
are-are you gonna stay
that jacked forever?

20:31.556 --> 20:33.165
I don't know. Do you like it?

20:33.166 --> 20:35.483
I-I-I do and I don't, you know?

20:35.484 --> 20:38.069
It-it was... it was nice
being held up against a wall,

20:38.070 --> 20:41.364
but when you made me suck
the hairdryer, I-I...

20:41.365 --> 20:42.782
I enjoyed it, I did,

20:42.783 --> 20:45.702
but I-I don't want to do
that specific thing again.

20:45.703 --> 20:47.871
Well, that's too bad,
'cause I'm ready for round two.

20:47.872 --> 20:50.374
Just got to make sure
the kids don't come in.

20:53.210 --> 20:55.086
Now no one can get in,

20:55.087 --> 20:56.588
and no one can get out.

20:56.589 --> 20:58.214
Hands on the headboard, chief.

20:58.215 --> 20:59.633
Yes, ma'am.